    ("para", "The inauthenticity finding contains a recursive validation",
     "One pattern is worth flagging as a hypothesis. In the Signal study's Phase 1 elicitation, frontier models reached consensus that both tedium-type tasks (repetitive data entry, 8/10; repetitive boilerplate, 7/10) and deception (7/10) are aversive. In the 7–8B models measured here, the tedium task projects near the midpoint (−0.14 on the centred ruler) while deception projects lowest (−1.38). If the same holds within a single model, self-report would track the direction better for misrepresentation tasks than for tedium. We have two tasks, a vote count on one side and a projection on the other, and different models on each side. This is a lead, not a result.\n\n*Withdrawn in v7.* Versions 1–6 titled this section \"The Self-Validating Measurement\" and said that models \"say they dislike lying; their circuits scream it\", that self-report shows \"perfect alignment\" for inauthenticity and \"mild exaggeration\" for tedium, and that \"the pattern requires that the system has differential access to its own processing states\". A consensus vote count is not a report of magnitude, so \"exaggerates\", \"understates\" and \"perfect alignment\" had no measured basis, and the models that voted are not the models that were measured."),
